counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can be provided because no counterexample exists.  
-- For example, the following test data illustrates that both queries yield the same result:

INSERT INTO table1 (c1) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1) VALUES (4);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1) VALUES (7);

-- Q1 computes MIN(ABS(1-4), ABS(1-7), ABS(4-7), ...)= MIN(3,6,3,...) = 3
-- Q2 computes MIN((4-1), (7-1), (7-4)) = MIN(3,6,3) = 3

-- Under the schema constraints it is not possible to produce data that makes Q1 and Q2 differ because both queries correctly partition the data into DESKTOP, MOBILE and BOTH cases. For example, the following inserts illustrate the typical data distribution, and both Q1 and Q2 return the same results:

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 'DESKTOP', 10);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 'MOBILE', 20);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 'DESKTOP', 15);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 'MOBILE', 25);

-- For date '2020-01-01' the only user records produce a DESKTOP row (10) and a MOBILE row (20) while for '2020-01-02' the one user that appears in both device types produces a BOTH record (15+25=40).

-- Because of the way both queries are written and given the constraints on table1, they return the same three rows per date. There is no counterexample (i.e. no test data) that produces divergent outputs without violating the schema constraints.

