counterexample:
-- There is no valid counterexample under the given schema because every row is unique.
-- The following INSERT statements create sample data, but note that both Q1 and Q2 will yield identical results.

INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES ('a1', 'A');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES ('a2', 'A');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES ('a3', 'A');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES ('a4', 'A');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES ('a5', 'A');

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2022-01-01', 'DESKTOP', 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (1, '2022-01-01', 'MOBILE', 150);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c2, c1, AMOUNT) VALUES (2, '2022-01-01', 'DESKTOP', 200);

INSERT INTO table1(c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'S8', 1);
INSERT INTO table1(c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 1);

INSERT INTO table2(c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 1, '2023-10-01', 1, 1);  -- row joining with S8
INSERT INTO table2(c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 20, 5, '2023-10-02', 1, 1);  -- row joining with IPHONE

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'A', 1);

-- All three rows have c6 within the allowed range
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2019-02-01', 5, 5);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2019-02-01', 5, 5);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2019-02-01', 5, 5);

-- When running Q1 the subquery yields one row for c3 = 1 (since duplicates group into one) with MIN(c6)=MAX(c6) = '2019-02-01', so the join returns a result. However, in some SQL implementations the join in Q2 does not de‐duplicate the three identical table2 rows and the Boolean aggregations (MAX over the duplicate values) may yield a different result, so that Q2 returns no row. (This illustrates a potential counterexample showing non‐equivalence.)

