counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can produce a counterexample since the two queries are semantically equivalent. You may use the following sample data to test, but note that both queries will return the same results.

INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2023-01-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (2, 20, 100, '2023-01-01');

-- Even if you add another group with the same c3 value and a different c4, the final DISTINCT result for c3 remains the same:

INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 30, 200, '2023-02-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (2, 40, 200, '2023-03-01');

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'S8', 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 1);

INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, NULL, '2023-10-10', 1, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 20, 100, '2023-10-11', 1, 1);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 100, 0);
-- Normally the primary key would prevent a duplicate on '2020-01-02' for the same user. For the counterexample, assume duplicates are allowed (or the PK is removed for testing):
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 102, 0);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-05', 200, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-06', 201, 0);

-- Under the schema provided no counterexample exists without violating the primary key constraint.
-- Any attempt to insert two rows for the same c4 and the same c2 (i.e. duplicate logins on a given day) would violate:
--   PRIMARY KEY (c4, c2)

-- For example, the following data produce the identical result in Q1 and Q2:

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 10, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 11, 0);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-05', 20, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-06', 21, 0);

-- For group 1, MIN_DATE = '2023-01-01' and there is one login on '2023-01-02' (which equals MIN_DATE + 1).
-- For group 2, MIN_DATE = '2023-01-05' and there is one login on '2023-01-06'.

-- In both cases the numerator is 1 for each group and the denominator is 2, so both Q1 and Q2 yield 0.50 (after rounding),
-- demonstrating that under the enforced PK the two queries are equivalent.

