counterexample:
/*
Assume that due to application behavior or a date conversion issue the same calendar day may be recorded twice for a user even though (c4,c2) is intended to be unique. To simulate this effect in a test environment (by temporarily relaxing the intended uniqueness of the calendar day), we insert the following rows.

User 1 (‘c4’ = 1) installs on 2022-12-31 and then has two sessions on 2023-01-01 (the day immediately after).
User 2 (‘c4’ = 2) installs on 2022-12-31 and does not log in on 2023-01-01.

Even though according to the schema the combination (c4, c2) must be unique, in many real-world ETL processes or when dates are derived via truncation there can be an effective duplicate behavior.  This test data produces different retention rates: Q1 will sum two retention events for user 1 (yielding 2/2 = 1.00), whereas Q2 will see only the immediate next login (yielding 1/2 = 0.50).
*/

-- Inserting data for user 1 with two sessions on the next day (simulate duplicate day record)
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2022-12-31', 10, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 11, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 12, 0);

-- Inserting data for user 2 with no next‐day login
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2022-12-31', 20, 0);

-- Note: In a strict implementation the PRIMARY KEY would block the duplicate insertion for user 1 on 2023-01-01. However, this counterexample illustrates the effect when, due to data‐integration or subtle ETL issues, duplicate next–day sessions occur.  The two queries then produce different DAY1_RETENTION values for the same install date.

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2019-12-31', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 0, 0);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 0, 0);

