counterexample:
-- Insert a row in table3 with c10 = 100 that has no red connection (will be returned by both queries)
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (100, 'BLUE', 1, 1, 'foo');

-- Insert a row in table3 with c10 = 200 that is supposed to be excluded by Q1 but not detected by Q2 (because the red connection is not visible in table3)
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (200, 'GREEN', 2, 2, 'bar');

-- For the red connection on value 200, insert a row in table2 and table1. The red connection exists in table2/table1 so that Q1’s subquery will count it
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (10, 300, 200, 'abc', 5);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (300, 'RED', 'xyz');

-- Additionally, to “hide" the red connection from Q2’s inner query, we do NOT insert any row in table3 that would join with the red connection (other than the outer row having 200). 
-- (Remember: Q2 finds red connections only if there is a row in table3 that participates in the red join; here the only row with 200 is the same one in the outer query, and many SQL engines will not use the outer row as part of the subquery processing.)

-- Final state:
--   • Row with c10=100 ('BLUE') has no red join; both queries return it.
--   • Row with c10=200 ('GREEN') has a red join (via table2/table1) so Q1 excludes it; but Q2 does not “see" a red row in table3 (apart from the outer row) and so it returns the row.

-- Thus, Q1 and Q2 return different results for the c10=200 case.

