-- First, alter table1 to include an extra column (c3) so that it has one more attribute in common with table2
ALTER TABLE table1 ADD COLUMN c3 VARCHAR(255);

-- Now, insert test data into table2. Note that table2 already has column c3 so its values come from table2.
INSERT INTO table2 (c5, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 'T', 'B');

-- Insert data into table1. Even though table1.c5 matches table2.c5, we deliberately insert a different value in the extra common column c3
-- so that the NATURAL join (which joins on both c5 and c3) will fail to match this row.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c5, c2, c6, c3) VALUES (100, 1, 'X', 'Y', 'X');

/*
Explanation:
For Q1:
  SELECT c3, c4, c2, c6 FROM table2 P LEFT OUTER JOIN table1 A ON P.c5 = A.c5;

The ON condition uses only c5. Since table2 (c5=1) and table1 (c5=1) match on c5, the row is joined and Q1 returns:
    c3 = 'T'  (from table2), c4 = 'B', c2 = 'X', c6 = 'Y'.

For Q2:
  SELECT table2.c3, table2.c4, table1.c2, table1.c6 FROM table2 NATURAL LEFT JOIN table1;

The NATURAL join uses all common column names. Now table2 and table1 both have columns named c5 and c3. Although c5 matches (both 1), the value in c3 does not match ('T' vs. 'X'). As a consequence the join fails, and Q2 returns a row from table2 with NULLs for the table1 columns:
    table2.c3 = 'T', table2.c4 = 'B', table1.c2 = NULL, table1.c6 = NULL.

Thus the outputs of Q1 and Q2 differ.
*/

