counterexample:
-- Although one might try to force a difference by inserting a row with NULL, note that the overall aggregation makes the queries equivalent.
-- Here is an example INSERT script that can be run on the schema. Both queries will return the same output.

INSERT INTO table1(c1) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO table1(c1) VALUES (NULL);
INSERT INTO table1(c1) VALUES (2);
INSERT INTO table1(c1) VALUES (2);

-- Explanation of test data:
-- • The row with 1 appears exactly once and qualifies in both queries.
-- • The row with NULL appears exactly once. It qualifies in the Q2 subquery (COUNT(*)=1) but not in Q1 (COUNT(c1)=0).
-- • The two rows with 2 are duplicates; hence the group for 2 does not qualify in either query.
-- The subquery in Q1 returns only value 1, so MAX(c1)=1.
-- The subquery in Q2 returns both 1 and NULL. However, MAX ignores the NULL and returns 1.

-- As a result, both queries return 1. This demonstrates that no counterexample exists where the outputs of Q1 and Q2 differ.

