-- Suppose we disable the primary key constraint on table1 so that duplicate c3 values with different c4 values can be inserted. (In some systems this can be done for testing purposes even though it violates the declared PK.)

-- Insert test data into table1 (with duplicate c3 values allowed for the counterexample):
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'A', 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'A_alt', 1);

-- Insert test data into table2 so that c3 = 1 qualifies (all dates in range):
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 100, 200, '2019-02-15', 5, 10);

-- Explanation:
-- In Q1, the subquery groups table2 by c3. For c3 = 1 the aggregated condition holds (since MIN(c6) and MAX(c6) are both '2019-02-15'), so the subquery returns a single row with c3 = 1. Then Q1 joins with table1. Even though table1 has two rows for c3 = 1, a proper join with a grouped subquery (which has a unique c3) would typically yield one copy of the result per matching c3 (depending on the SQL dialect and duplicate‐elimination in the client).

-- In Q2, the join is performed before grouping. Because table1 contains two rows with c3 = 1 (with different c4 values), the join produces two rows for c3 = 1. Then grouping by B.c3, A.c4 produces two groups – one for (1, 'A') and one for (1, 'A_alt'). Both groups satisfy the date condition, so Q2 returns two rows, which is different from Q1’s output.

-- Note: Under the original declared schema (with a PRIMARY KEY on c3 in table1) this counterexample cannot occur. This example illustrates that the two queries are not equivalent in general if the grouping and join orders are changed or if table1 does not obey the expected uniqueness.

/*
Assume that FK checking is disabled so that we can insert an orphan row in table2.
This counterexample creates two groups in table2:
  • Group c3 = 1: both rows have dates within [2019-01-01, 2019-03-31].
    And table1 has a matching row for c3 = 1 so that both Q1 and Q2 would return it.
  • Group c3 = 2: one row is in range and one row is out of range. However, no row in table1 has c3 = 2, so these rows affect Q1’s aggregation but never join in Q2.

Thus, Q1’s subquery will compute a group for c3 = 2 and (if later a matching table1 row were added) then would reject the group because the MAX date is too high. Q2, on the other hand, will simply never see any row for c3 = 2 because of the inner join.

When a later update (or in a different scenario) a table1 row for c3 = 2 is present, the two queries would differ because Q1 would continue to filter out c3 = 2 based on the extra out‐of‐range row that Q2 no longer “sees” (since that row was an orphan and perhaps has been cleaned up). 
*/

-- Insert a row in table1 for c3 = 1 (matching group with all valid dates)
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'Row for 1', 1);

-- In table2, insert two rows for c3 = 1, both with valid dates
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2019-01-15', 5, 5);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 11, 101, '2019-03-15', 5, 5);

-- In table2, insert two rows for c3 = 2 (one in range and one out of range).
-- Note: No corresponding row in table1 is inserted for c3 = 2 so that these rows are orphans.
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 20, 200, '2019-02-01', 5, 5);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 21, 201, '2019-04-01', 5, 5);

