INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 20, 0);

/*
Expected results:
  • Q1 groups by installation date and uses COUNT(DISTINCT c4) so it sees 2 installs and a total retention numerator of 2 (device 1 contributes 2 and device 2 contributes 1 matching login) leading to a computed day1 retention of 2/2 = 1.00.
  • Q2’s LEFT JOIN returns 3 rows for install date 2020-01-01 (2 rows for device 1 and 1 row for device 2), so COUNT(I.INSTALL_DATE) = 3 while COUNT(A.c2) = 2, yielding a day1 retention of 2/3 ≈ 0.67.
This demonstrates that Q1 and Q2 are not equivalent if duplicate login records on the day following installation exist.

Note: Under the declared schema (which enforces a PK on (c4,c2)), such duplicates cannot occur. This counterexample is useful to illustrate the subtle differences in the aggregation logic when duplicate join rows are possible due to data anomalies or relaxed constraints.
*/

