/* Insert data into table1 */
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES
  (1, 'S8', 1),
  (2, 'IPHONE', 1);

/* In table2, insert one row that will join with the 'S8' row and provide a candidate (with c1 = 100),
   and one row that will join with the 'IPHONE' row but has c1 = NULL. */
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES
  (1, 10, 100, '2020-01-01', 1, 1),
  (2, 20, NULL, '2020-01-02', 1, 1);

/* Under these inserts:
   • The main query of Q1 (which is based on an inner join) finds the candidate value 100 via the join of table1 row 1 and table2 row (first row).
   • Its subquery (also using an inner join) looks for rows with table1.c4 = 'IPHONE'. However, the only row joining to the IPHONE row is the second one, and because c1 is NULL that row is returned by the subquery only as NULL.
      Since an expression like 100 NOT IN (NULL) evaluates to UNKNOWN, the candidate 100 passes the condition in Q1 (because the inner join “drops” the problematic NULL row when it comes to matching non‐NULL candidate values).
   • By contrast, Q2’s subquery uses a LEFT JOIN and thus returns the NULL value from the IPHONE branch. Then 100 NOT IN (NULL) evaluates to UNKNOWN, so Q2 eliminates the candidate value.

   Therefore, Q1 returns 100 while Q2 returns an empty set. */

