counterexample:
/* For the purpose of this counterexample, we assume that foreign key checks are temporarily disabled. In MySQL you could do the following: */

SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS=0;

-- Insert a valid table1 row
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 30);

-- Insert a table2 row that matches table1
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (100, 1);

-- Insert a table2 row that does NOT match any row in table1
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (200, 2);

SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS=1;

/*
Expected behavior:
For Q1 (inner join): Only the row from table2 with c1=1 will be joined with table1, so the output will be:
   c4 | AVERAGE_YEARS
  100 | 30.00
The row with c4=200 is dropped because there is no matching table1 row.

For Q2 (LEFT join): Both table2 rows appear. The row with c4=100 gets a valid AVG(c2)=30.00. The row with c4=200 has no match in table1 so c2 is NULL, and AVG(NULL) yields NULL. Thus, the output will include:
   c4 | AVERAGE_YEARS
  100 | 30.00
  200 | NULL
*/

