/*
Assume for this counterexample that the application might allow duplicate rows for the same (c4, c2) even though the declared PRIMARY KEY would normally forbid it. (In practice the PK ensures there is at most one row per date per group. However, if the data‐quality rules are relaxed or if the business logic accidentally permits duplicate consecutive dates, then Q1 and Q2 produce different results.)

We create two groups:
  • Group 1 (c4 = 1) qualifies – and it has two rows with 2020-01-02 (the consecutive day for the minimum 2020-01-01).
  • Group 2 (c4 = 2) does not qualify (only one row is provided).

For Q1 the join for group 1 returns two rows that satisfy (MIN(c2)+1 = A.c2) so the SUM becomes 2, while the denominator (COUNT(DISTINCT c4)) is 2 (counting groups 1 and 2) giving a fraction 2/2 = 1.00. In Q2, however, only the row with RNK = 1 in group 1 is examined (its LEAD is 2020-01-02) so group 1 is counted only once; then the fraction becomes 1/2 = 0.50.
*/

