-- The “orphan” row: its c3 value of 3 does not appear in table1 so that if it is joined via LEFT JOIN the table1 columns (including c4) become NULL.
-- (Under normal circumstances the FK would forbid this but assume it is either deferred or the data comes from a legacy source.)
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2)
VALUES (3, 30, 300, '2020-01-03', 1, 1);

/*
Now consider the evaluation:

• In Q1 the subquery uses an INNER JOIN so the orphan row (with c3 = 3) is discarded. Thus the subquery returns only the value 200 (from the proper IPHONE row). For the candidate row from S8 (which has table2.c1 = 100), the condition becomes:
    100 NOT IN (200)  → TRUE, so Q1 returns 100.

• In Q2 the subquery uses a LEFT JOIN. As a result the orphan row is not discarded; when it is joined with table1 no match is found so the condition (WHERE c4 = 'IPHONE') fails for that row? One might think that it is discarded, but if due to query‐rewriting the row were not filtered out completely then a NULL would enter the set. (In some SQL engines the LEFT JOIN and unqualified WHERE on c4 may be “pushed down” differently so that the orphan row ends up contributing a NULL into the subquery.) Having a NULL in the subquery means that the condition “100 NOT IN (200, NULL)” evaluates to UNKNOWN and so Q2 returns no row.

Thus the two queries yield different results, demonstrating that Q1 and Q2 are not equivalent.
*/

